<Doc
  title="Scores and feedback"
  description="How mcpd records machine evaluation and human judgement, why they are stored apart, and the query that finds the runs a scorer marked down."
  lede="A score is what a machine thought. Feedback is what a person thought. mcpd keeps them as two signals and never averages one into the other."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>Two signals, not one number</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>score</strong> is a machine evaluation: a judge model, a regular
    expression, a unit test, anything that can produce a number about a run. A
    <strong>feedback</strong> record is a person's judgement: a thumbs-down in
    your product, a reviewer's rating, a corrected answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both attach to a <code>trace_id</code> or a <code>span_id</code>, both
    arrive as signals in their own right, and both are stored in their own
    table. They are
    never combined. A judge scoring 0.9 for relevance and a customer pressing
    thumbs-down are both true statements, and the disagreement between them is
    the most useful thing on the run. An average would erase exactly that, and
    leave you with a number nobody can act on.
  </p>

  <SignalFlow class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h2>A score declares its scale</h2>
  <p>
    <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> are part of the score. Without them a
    value is not interpretable: 0.86 could be a good result on a 0 to 1 scale,
    a poor one on a 0 to 5 scale, or a distance where lower is better. Six
    months later, with two scorers in the same project, nobody can tell which.
    The spec defaults them to 0 and 1, and every SDK requires them at the call
    site so that a default is never accepted by accident.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>scorer_id</code></td>
        <td>Which scorer produced this, such as <code>answer_relevance</code>. Required.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>scorer_version</code></td>
        <td>A score compares only to another score from the same version. Change the prompt of a judge and you have a new scorer, not a continuation of the old one.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>value</code></td>
        <td>The number.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>min</code>, <code>max</code></td>
        <td>The declared scale the number sits on.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>label</code></td>
        <td>A categorical result, when the scorer produces one alongside the number.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>reason</code></td>
        <td>Why. For a judge model this is its explanation, and it is what makes a score arguable instead of magic.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>source</code></td>
        <td>Live, offline, experiment or manual.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>score_trace_id</code></td>
        <td>The trace of the scoring run itself, so a suspicious score can be opened and read like any other run.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>cost</code></td>
        <td>What producing the score cost.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Recording one</h2>
  <p>
    The SDK helper queues a score the same way it queues a span, so the call
    does not block and cannot throw. With no <code>traceId</code> given it
    attaches to the active span's trace.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" region="score" title="score and feedback" />

  <p>
    Over HTTP that is <code>POST /v1/api/scores</code>, taking a
    <code>Score</code> as the body. A score must name the
    <code>trace_id</code> or the <code>span_id</code> it is about; a score
    attached to nothing is refused with 400 rather than stored where it can
    never be found. Send <code>score_id</code> as an empty string and the
    server generates one.
  </p>

  <h2>Where a score came from</h2>
  <p>
    <code>source</code> is separate from <code>scorer_id</code> because the
    same scorer runs in more than one context, and the contexts are not
    comparable. Re-scoring last week's traffic with today's judge is not
    evidence that quality improved.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Source</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>live</code></td><td>Produced online, as the traced run happened.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>offline</code></td><td>Produced later, by scoring stored traces.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>experiment</code></td><td>Produced by an experiment run against a dataset.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>manual</code></td><td>Entered by a person, through the API or the interface.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    A score written through an SDK defaults to <code>live</code>. Set it
    yourself for the other three.
  </p>

  <h2>Scoring costs money, so it is recorded</h2>
  <p>
    A judged evaluation is usually another model call. Scoring every production
    run with a large judge can cost more than serving the traffic did, and that
    is a bill people discover late. A <code>Score</code> therefore carries a
    <code>cost</code> in the same shape a span does, with a currency, an input
    and output split, and the price book that produced the figure. Set it when
    you write the score through the HTTP API or a native ingest batch.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>score_trace_id</code> is the other half of the same idea. Trace the
    scoring run itself and the id goes on the score, so a judge that started
    marking everything down can be opened, read, and shown to be broken. The
    daemon&rsquo;s own judge does this for every score it produces.
  </p>

  <h2>The review queue</h2>
  <p>
    Runs a person should look at go in the review queue:
    <code>POST /v1/api/review-queue</code> with the trace id, and optionally
    the step, the feedback that prompted it, an assignee and a note. An item
    starts <code>pending</code>; <code>PATCH /v1/api/review-queue/{'{id}'}</code>
    assigns it, or marks it <code>reviewed</code> or <code>dismissed</code> with
    what the reviewer found. Every change is a new version of the item, so who
    decided what, and when, is kept. <code>GET /v1/api/review-queue</code>
    filters on status and assignee and counts what is still pending. The Review
    screen in the app works through it, and a run can be queued from the run
    panel.
  </p>

  <h2>Human feedback</h2>
  <p>
    Feedback is written from your own product. Call it from the control the
    person actually pressed and the judgement lands on the exact trace that
    produced the answer they were reacting to. <code>kind</code> decides the
    shape of <code>value</code>.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Kind</th><th><code>value</code></th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>thumbs</code></td><td><code>up</code> or <code>down</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>rating</code></td><td>A number, on a scale the producer declares.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>correction</code></td><td>The right answer, as text.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tag</code></td><td>A label chosen from a set.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>comment</code></td><td>Free prose, with no score attached.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="a thumbs-down from your product"
    samples={[
      {
        lang: 'typescript',
        label: 'TypeScript',
        shiki: 'typescript',
        code: `// No traceId: it attaches to the active span's trace.
mcpd.feedback({
  kind: 'thumbs',
  value: 'down',
  comment: 'Named the wrong depot.',
  authorKind: 'end_user',
})`,
      },
      {
        lang: 'curl',
        label: 'cURL',
        shiki: 'bash',
        code: `curl -s -X POST "$MCPD/v1/api/feedback" \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d "{
    \\"feedback_id\\": \\"\\",
    \\"trace_id\\": \\"$TRACE\\",
    \\"kind\\": \\"thumbs\\",
    \\"value\\": \\"down\\",
    \\"comment\\": \\"Named the wrong depot.\\",
    \\"author_kind\\": \\"end_user\\",
    \\"timestamp\\": \\"$NOW\\",
    \\"correlation\\": { \\"project\\": \\"default\\" }
  }"`,
      },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Redaction matches on key names, and it runs over the <code>metadata</code>
    you attach to a judgement. <code>author_id</code> is stored as you send it,
    so send a pseudonymous id rather than an email address. See
    <a href="/docs/privacy" class="link">Redaction and retention</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Scoring what the run did, not what it said</h2>
  <p>
    Most scorers read a run's answer. An agent that finishes with <em>I ran all
    the tests and there were no regressions</em> satisfies every one of them,
    and satisfies a judge model too, because the sentence is well formed and
    says the right thing. The sentence is the agent's account of its own work,
    and an account is not evidence. If the test command never ran, no scorer
    that reads the answer can tell you so.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two built-in scorers read the trace instead. They ignore the answer
    entirely and ask what the run recorded.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Scorer</th><th>Scores 1 when</th><th>Configured with</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>span_present</code></td>
        <td>The run recorded at least one span of the required kind or name.</td>
        <td><code>span_kind</code>, <code>span_name</code>, or both together. Set both and both must hold.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>tool_succeeded</code></td>
        <td>A tool span with that name ran, and at least one call of it ended <code>ok</code>.</td>
        <td><code>tool_name</code>.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Both need configuration, so neither runs in the default set. A scorer that
    passed because nobody told it what to look for would report every run as
    having done work no one checked, which is the failure it exists to catch.
    Name them, and give them their settings:
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "trace_ids": ["4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736"],
  "scorers": ["tool_succeeded", "span_present"],
  "config": {
    "tool_name": "run_tests",
    "span_kind": "tool_call",
    "span_name": "run_tests"
  }
}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>span_name</code> and <code>tool_name</code> are compared exactly, and
    <code>case_sensitive</code> does not loosen them. A span name is an
    identifier your code chose rather than prose, and a loose comparison would
    let <code>run_tests_dry</code> satisfy an assertion about
    <code>run_tests</code>, on precisely the run the check was written for.
    <code>tool_succeeded</code> accepts any of the four tool span kinds,
    <code>tool_call</code>, <code>client_tool_call</code>,
    <code>provider_tool_call</code> and <code>mcp_tool_call</code>, because
    which one a run records says where the tool lives, not whether it ran.
  </p>

  <h3>Two ways to score zero, and they are different bugs</h3>
  <p>
    A tool that never ran and a tool that ran and failed are both a zero, and
    fixing them are opposite jobs: one is a step the agent skipped, the other a
    step that broke. The <code>reason</code> separates them, and carries the
    recorded error so the score is actionable without opening the run.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What happened</th><th><code>reason</code></th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>The tool never ran.</td>
        <td><code>"run_tests" never ran; the run records 2 tool call(s), named: lookup_order, send_email</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>It ran and failed.</td>
        <td><code>"run_tests" ran 1 time(s) and none ended ok; the last ended error: pytest exited 1: 3 failed</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>It failed, was retried, and worked.</td>
        <td><code>"run_tests" ran 2 time(s), 1 ended ok and 1 did not</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    <code>span_present</code> does the same thing for its own zero: it names
    what it looked for and then what the run actually holds, because a wrong
    name and absent instrumentation are indistinguishable from a bare
    <em>not found</em> and have nothing in common as fixes.
  </p>

  <h3>What these scorers prove, and what they do not</h3>
  <p>
    They prove an action was <strong>recorded</strong>. They do not prove it was
    correct. A <code>run_tests</code> span that ended <code>ok</code> says the
    tool returned without raising, not that it ran the right suite, not that it
    ran the whole suite, and not that anything read the result. Treating a green
    <code>tool_succeeded</code> as proof the code works is the same mistake as
    trusting the sentence, one step further along.
  </p>
  <p>
    They also measure the trace rather than the world. A span exists because
    your process recorded one. Work that happened inside a step you never
    instrumented is invisible here, and reads exactly like work that never
    happened, which is the one way these scorers report a failure that is not
    one. When one of them scores zero, check the instrumentation before you
    conclude the agent lied.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both hold even when payload capture is off. Neither reads an input, an
    output or a completion: they read span kind, span name and span status,
    which the daemon holds whatever your capture settings are.
  </p>

  <h2>The query that finds bad answers</h2>
  <p>
    This is what the scores are for. <code>has_score</code> names a scorer and
    <code>score_below</code> sets the bar, and the trace list returns the runs
    that scorer put underneath it. The comparison is strict, so
    <code>score_below=0.5</code> excludes a run that scored exactly 0.5.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" region="query" title="find the runs that scored badly" />

  <p>
    <code>score_below</code> only means something with <code>has_score</code>
    beside it: a bar with no scorer named is a bar on nothing. Both are
    parameters of <code>GET /v1/api/traces</code>, so they combine with every
    other filter there. Bad answers from one model, in production, in the last
    day, is one request.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading them back</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/scores</code> lists machine evaluations, filtered by
    <code>scorer_id</code>, <code>source</code>, <code>trace_id</code>,
    <code>experiment_id</code>, <code>min_value</code>, <code>max_value</code>
    and a time window, with cursor paging. <code>source</code> takes
    <code>live</code>, <code>offline</code>, <code>experiment</code> or
    <code>manual</code>, which is how you read back the judge's own scores
    without the ones a person typed.
    <code>GET /v1/api/feedback</code> takes a <code>trace_id</code> and returns
    the judgements on that run. Always name the run: with no
    <code>trace_id</code> the endpoint refuses with a 400 naming the parameter,
    rather than an empty list that would read as though nobody had judged
    anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    The score list takes no <code>order</code>: it is newest first, and its
    cursor compares one way only. The run and span lists do take one, so it is
    refused here by name rather than accepted and dropped. The same applies to
    the <code>kind</code>, <code>from</code>, <code>to</code> and
    <code>cursor</code> filters that several SDK clients will let you set on a
    feedback query: all four are refused by name.
  </p>
  <p>
    To read a run and everything attached to it at once, ask for the extras by
    name: <code>GET /v1/api/traces/{'{trace_id}'}?include=scores,feedback</code>,
    or <code>include=all</code> for the logs as well.
  </p>

  <h2>What the trace list carries</h2>
  <p>
    Each stored trace keeps two rollups so the list needs no join. One is the
    mean value <em>per scorer</em>: an average within
    <code>answer_relevance</code>, and a separate average within
    <code>groundedness</code>. The other is a count of feedback
    <em>per kind</em>: three thumbs, one correction. Counts, not a mean, because
    the mean of a thumbs-up and a correction is not a quantity.
  </p>
  <p>
    mcpd keeps each score separate, by design. A single
    figure combining a judge model, a regular expression and a customer's
    thumbs-down would be a number with no unit and no way to argue with it.
    mcpd shows you the parts and lets you decide.
  </p>

